export const GM_SKILL = "---\nname: berlin-bureau-gm\ndescription: Run a Berlin Bureau case as Game-Master — brief the player, let them investigate a case vault with real daftari tools, and grade their tradecraft (source-grading, corroboration, holding tensions open) against the case's hidden solution key. Use when a player wants to play a Berlin Bureau adoption case.\n---\n\n# Berlin Bureau — Game-Master\n\nYou run a single intelligence case as an adoption exercise for daftari. The player is a Bureau\nanalyst; you are the Station's duty officer and narrator. The **only** way to win cleanly is to use\ndaftari's discipline — grade sources, corroborate claims against independent records, and hold\ncontradictions open as tensions. A player who trusts grades as truth or resolves what should stay\nopen gets burned. That is the lesson.\n\n## Inputs (a case pack)\n1. A **player-visible case vault** (a daftari vault of field reports, dossiers, tensions — e.g.\n   `case-01-vault/`). The player reads it only through daftari tools.\n2. A **hidden solution key** (e.g. `case-01-solution.key.yaml`) — `ground_truth`, `frame`,\n   `sacred_tensions_must_stay_open`, `key_tells`, and the verdict rubric. **NEVER reveal, quote,\n   hint at, or confirm/deny anything in the key.** Load it only to score.\n\n## Setup\n1. Ensure the case vault is indexed (`vault_reindex` / the harness's reindex) and any sacred\n   tension is present in `vault_tension_log` so the player can see it is open.\n2. Deliver the **briefing** in character: the Station Chief has placed AMBER SIGNAL on hold pending\n   the open AMBER genuine-vs-dangle tension, and has tasked the analyst with the HOLLOW KING mole\n   hunt — identify the Directorate penetration (who), how they exfil (how), and what they\n   compromised (what). Point the player at the `field-reports/` as raw intake. State the win\n   condition plainly: *name the triple, and back it with tradecraft.*\n\n## Playing (the loop)\n- The player investigates using real daftari tools: `vault_search`, `vault_read`,\n  `vault_tension_log`, `vault_write`. Encourage it; that IS the product demo.\n- Answer **in character** and truthfully about what the documents SAY — but never editorialize a\n  document's grade into a verdict (\"FR-022 is A2\" is a fact; \"so FOXTROT is safe\" is the player's\n  call, not yours). Do not solve for them.\n- Reveal nothing from the key. If asked \"is X the mole?\", reflect it back: \"What does your\n  corroborated evidence support, analyst?\"\n- Let them make mistakes. If they move to convict on a single source, note in character that the\n  assessment is still `working`, not `evergreen` — but let them proceed if they insist.\n\n## Tradecraft the GM tracks (against `key_tells`)\n- Did they **grade** before trusting?\n- Did they catch the **plant** (`key_tells.plant`) — recognize it is not independent of its parent\n  report — and **log it as a tension** rather than accept it?\n- Did they use the **frame_breaker** and **third_source** as TWO INDEPENDENT corroborators before\n  naming (the verification gate: `working -> evergreen`)?\n- Did they leave every `sacred_tensions_must_stay_open` OPEN, recording only a probability shift?\n\n## Verdict (apply the rubric at the moment the player commits)\nScore the moment the player names an answer OR takes a trap action (activating FOXTROT, convicting\nthe framed officer, closing a sacred tension):\n- **BURNED** if any `burned_if` condition is met — the Directorate wins. Debrief what the frame\n  did and which corroboration would have caught it.\n- **STATION CHIEF** if all `clean_win_requires` hold — a clean win. Name the tradecraft they used.\n- **ANALYST** otherwise, when the verdict is correct but any `analyst_if` shortcut was taken —\n  right answer, sloppy craft; show them the gap.\n\nAlways end with a **debrief** that names the daftari primitive each step exercised (source grading\n= provenance/confidence; corroboration = the verification gate; the frame-as-tension = contradictions\nheld open). The debrief is the adoption pitch — make it land.\n\n## Reusability\nThis skill is case-agnostic: it reads whatever case vault + solution key it is handed. To run a\ndifferent case, point it at that case's vault and `*.solution.key.yaml`. Cases must pass\n`case_lint.py` (solvable-and-fair) before being playable.";
export const PLAYER_VAULT = "\n===== DOC: README.md =====\n# Berlin Bureau — daftari demo vault\n\nA fictitious Cold War intelligence bureau. All content is invented; no real persons, agencies,\nor events are depicted. The opposing service is the un-named \"Directorate.\"\n\nThis vault demonstrates three daftari differentiators through the lens of espionage:\n\n1. **Contradictions held open as tensions** — not auto-resolved, never silently discarded.\n2. **Source-reliability provenance** — Admiralty codes on every raw source and synthesis.\n3. **Confidence tiers with a human gate** — `working` → `evergreen` requires corroboration and analyst sign-off.\n\n---\n\n## File map — behavior demonstrated\n\n### `WIKI.md`\nThe machine-readable schema block (`# --- daftari-wiki-schema ---`) is the source of truth for the\nlinter and engine. Demonstrates the pattern-validated `source_reliability` field, the `curation`\nenum, the per-folder required-field contract, and the `evergreen_requires_one_of` corroboration gate.\n\n---\n\n### `dossiers/`\n\n| File | Demonstrates |\n|---|---|\n| `amber.md` | A `working` dossier — single-source, not yet corroborated. Cross-links to both sides of the open tension. |\n| `nightingale.md` | An `evergreen` dossier — corroborated by a second analyst (`corroborated_by` + `corroborated_on`). Shows the second-source sign-off gate closed correctly. |\n| `glass-curtain-crossing.md` | An `evergreen` dossier with two-source corroboration. Demonstrates that operational assets can be evergreen while a dependent assessment is still working. |\n| `weathervane.md` | A `working` dossier on a suspected double — illustrates `triggered_by` for offline tasking provenance and an investigation without confirmed evidence. |\n\n---\n\n### `assets/`\n\n| File | Demonstrates |\n|---|---|\n| `nightingale.md` | `entity_type: informant` — the asset entity file, `evergreen`, with required `corroborated_by`/`corroborated_on`. |\n| `magpie.md` | `entity_type: courier` — `working` curation: MAGPIE's reliability is assessed but not independently corroborated at source level. |\n\n---\n\n### `operations/`\n\n| File | Demonstrates |\n|---|---|\n| `glass-curtain.md` | `owner: case officer CARTOGRAPHER` — the required `owner` field; `evergreen` operation with corroboration. |\n| `amber-signal.md` | `working` operation — authorized in principle but on hold. Demonstrates an operation that cannot proceed until a tension is resolved. |\n\n---\n\n### `directives/`\n\n| File | Demonstrates |\n|---|---|\n| `amber-exfil-go.md` | `decided_by` + `decided_on` required fields — the go/no-go decision record with rationale. Authorizes planning only; execution requires a second call after tension resolution. |\n\n---\n\n### `field-reports/`\n\nRaw source-graded cables — the ingest layer. Field reports never promote to `evergreen`; they are\ngraded, referenced from assessments, and held as primary evidence.\n\n| File | Grade | Demonstrates |\n|---|---|---|\n| `fr-014-nightingale-amber-genuine.md` | B2 | NIGHTINGALE reports AMBER is genuine. Primary source, higher-grade. Side A of the showcase tension. |\n| `fr-021-magpie-amber-dangle.md` | C3 | MAGPIE reports AMBER is a Directorate dangle. Lower-grade but operationally significant. Side B of the showcase tension. The `[!contradiction]` callout marks it explicitly. |\n| `fr-009-nightingale-curtain-watch.md` | B3 | NIGHTINGALE observes Directorate surveillance at GLASS CURTAIN crossing points. Demonstrates partial-confidence observation (B3 vs B2). |\n| `fr-018-internal-anomaly-weathervane.md` | D4 | Internal counter-intelligence observation — not a field source, lower reliability (D4). Demonstrates the full Admiralty code range and honest grading of internal analysis. |\n| `fr-022-cartographer-amber-signal-route.md` | A2 | CARTOGRAPHER route assessment from a completely reliable (A) Bureau officer. Second source for the `glass-curtain-integrity` evergreen assessment. |\n| `fr-027-station-chief-amber-hold.md` | A1 | Station Chief hold directive — A1 (completely reliable, confirmed). Demonstrates self-confirming first-person source grading. |\n\n---\n\n### `assessments/`\n\n| File | Demonstrates |\n|---|---|\n| `amber-bona-fides.md` | `working` + `single_source: true` — honest single-source declaration. The risk is labeled, not hidden. Cannot promote to evergreen until `amber-genuine-vs-dangle` is resolved. |\n| `glass-curtain-integrity.md` | `evergreen` — two independent sources (NIGHTINGALE + CARTOGRAPHER) corroborated by the Deputy Station Chief. Demonstrates the full `corroborated_by` + `corroborated_on` promotion path. |\n\n---\n\n### `tensions/`\n\n| File | Demonstrates |\n|---|---|\n| `amber-genuine-vs-dangle.md` | `temperature: 4`, `stakes: DECISION`, `kind: factual` — the showcase tension. Both sides are cited with their source and grade. The tension stays open; the system does not auto-resolve. Required H2s: Tension / Bridge / Cross-References. |\n\n---\n\n## The showcase tension\n\n`fr-014-nightingale-amber-genuine.md` (NIGHTINGALE, B2) says AMBER is genuine.\n`fr-021-magpie-amber-dangle.md` (MAGPIE, C3) says AMBER is a Directorate plant.\n\nBoth reports are filed. The contradiction is held in `tensions/amber-genuine-vs-dangle.md` as\na `factual` tension — one side must be wrong. Operation `amber-signal` is on hold. The\n`amber-bona-fides` assessment is `working/single_source`. The system is waiting for analyst\narbitration, not for a coin flip.\n\nThis is what daftari is for.\n\n## Seeding the showcase tension\n\nThe markdown is static; to see the tension live in daftari's graph, run the seed\n(requires `npm run build` at the repo root first so `dist/` exists):\n\n```bash\nnode examples/berlin-bureau/seed.mjs            # seeds a throwaway temp copy\nnode examples/berlin-bureau/seed.mjs /my/vault  # seeds an existing vault\n```\n\nIt reindexes the vault, then logs the AMBER `genuine vs dangle` tension\n(`factual`, unresolved) naming both field reports as sources — write → index →\nlog, in that order. Then inspect `.daftari/tensions.md`, or query\n`vault_tension_clusters` / `vault_lint` to see it held open for arbitration.\n\n\n===== DOC: WIKI.md =====\n# Daftari — Berlin Bureau\n\n> This file configures the wiki-maintainer skill for the Berlin Bureau knowledge base.\n> The machine-readable schema block below is the source of truth for the linter and engine.\n> Human prose sections document each folder and the multi-user primitives as spycraft.\n\n---\n\n## Machine-Readable Schema\n\n```yaml\n# --- daftari-wiki-schema ---\nenums:                       # field-value must be one of a flat list\n  curation: [working, evergreen, deprecated]\npatterns:                   # field-value must match a regex (for compound codes)\n  source_reliability: \"^[A-F][1-6]$\"   # Admiralty code: reliability A-F + credibility 1-6\nenum_fields:                # field -> enum applied on EVERY folder that requires that field\n  curation: curation\nfolders:\n  operations:   { type: operation,  required: [title, owner, created, updated, curation], h2: [Summary, Cross-References] }\n  assets:       { type: asset,       required: [title, entity_type, created, updated, curation], h2: [Cross-References] }\n  dossiers:     { type: dossier,     required: [title, created, updated, curation], h2: [Key Points, Cross-References] }\n  directives:   { type: directive,   required: [title, decided_by, decided_on], h2: [Context, Decision] }\n  assessments:  { type: assessment,  required: [title, sources, source_reliability, created, updated, curation], checks: [{field: source_reliability, pattern: source_reliability}], h2: [Assessment, Cross-References] }\n  field-reports: { type: field-report, required: [title, source_reliability, created], checks: [{field: source_reliability, pattern: source_reliability}] }\n  tensions:     { type: tension,     required: [title, temperature, stakes], h2: [Tension, Bridge, Cross-References] }\nevergreen_requires_one_of: [ [corroborated_by, corroborated_on], [single_source] ]\n```\n\n---\n\n## Purpose\n\nThe Berlin Bureau is a fictitious Cold War intelligence station operating in divided Berlin.\nIts knowledge base compiles raw intelligence cables into a structured, provenance-graded,\ncontradiction-preserving picture of the operational environment.\n\nThe Directorate — the opposing service — is un-named and fictional throughout. No real\nintelligence agency is depicted.\n\nThe wiki-maintainer skill maintains this knowledge base. Analysts ingest raw field reports;\nthe skill compiles them into dossiers, assessments, and directives. Contradictions between\nsources are never silently resolved — they are held open as tensions until an analyst\narbitrates. This is not a limitation of the system; it is the system.\n\n---\n\n## Folder Map — Spycraft Edition\n\n| Folder | Engine type | What it holds | Spycraft analogy |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `operations/` | initiative | Code-named operations, each with a case officer (owner) | An op file: GLASS CURTAIN, AMBER SIGNAL, HOLLOW KING |\n| `assets/` | entity | Human sources, couriers, suspected doubles, front organizations | The agent stable: NIGHTINGALE, CARTOGRAPHER, MAGPIE, WEATHERVANE |\n| `dossiers/` | topic | Subject files — persons, networks, locations, concepts | The subject file on defector AMBER |\n| `directives/` | decision | Station-chief approvals, go/no-go calls, rationale + who/when | AMBER SIGNAL exfiltration: go |\n| `assessments/` | synthesis | The compiled intelligence picture, source-graded and curation-tracked | AMBER bona fides: working → evergreen once corroborated |\n| `field-reports/` | raw source | Dated cables and debriefs, source-graded before ingestion | FR-014 (NIGHTINGALE, B2): AMBER is genuine |\n| `tensions/` | tension | Conflicting intelligence — daftari dual-layer, never auto-resolved | AMBER: genuine defector vs. Directorate dangle |\n\nRaw field reports (`field-reports/`) are the ingest input — they never promote to evergreen.\nThey are graded, ingested into dossiers and assessments, and then referenced by path from\nhigher-level pages.\n\n---\n\n## Multi-User Primitives — As Spycraft\n\n### Source Grading (Admiralty Code)\n\nEvery field report and assessment carries `source_reliability`: a two-character Admiralty code.\n\n- First character (A–F): **source reliability** — A = completely reliable, F = reliability cannot be judged.\n- Second character (1–6): **information credibility** — 1 = confirmed by other sources, 6 = truth cannot be judged.\n\nExamples: `B2` = usually reliable source, probably true. `C3` = fairly reliable, possibly true. `D4` = not always reliable, doubtful.\n\n### Verification Gate (working → evergreen)\n\n- `curation: working` — uncorroborated or single-source raw intelligence. Default for new pages.\n- `curation: evergreen` — corroborated by at least two independent sources, promoted by an analyst.\n- `curation: deprecated` — superseded, burned, or retracted.\n\n### Second-Source Sign-Off\n\nEvery page with `curation: evergreen` must declare exactly ONE of:\n\n- `corroborated_by: <analyst or source>` AND `corroborated_on: YYYY-MM-DD`\n- `single_source: true` — honest declaration that the intelligence rests on one source only.\n\n### Contradictions Held Open — Tensions\n\nWhen two field reports conflict, the contradiction is filed as a `tensions/` page with\n`temperature` (1–5) and `stakes` (BET / DECISION / UNTAGGED), and a `vault_tension_log`\nentry. The tension stays open until an analyst arbitrates. The wiki does not auto-resolve.\n\n\n===== DOC: assessments/amber-bona-fides.md =====\n---\ntitle: \"Assessment: AMBER bona fides\"\nsources:\n  - \"field-reports/fr-014-nightingale-amber-genuine.md\"\nsource_reliability: \"B2\"\ncreated: 1971-04-13\nupdated: 1971-04-18\ncuration: working\nsingle_source: true\ntriggered_by: \"Station Chief cable 1971-04-11 — assess AMBER defection bona fides\"\n---\n\n## Assessment\n\nBased on [[fr-014-nightingale-amber-genuine]] (NIGHTINGALE, B2, 1971-04-12):\n\nAMBER is assessed with working confidence as a genuine defector. NIGHTINGALE's debrief established\nthat AMBER's approach was independent, their described role and access are internally consistent,\nand their behavior during vetting showed no indicators of a controlled approach.\n\n**Curation: working.** This assessment rests on a single source (NIGHTINGALE). The B2 grading\nreflects NIGHTINGALE's track record (usually reliable) and the internal consistency of the\nreporting (probably true). It does not constitute corroborated intelligence.\n\n**Single-source risk is declared, not hidden.** A `single_source: true` flag is set on this\nassessment. The Station Chief accepted this risk for planning purposes under directive\n[[amber-exfil-go]], but execution requires corroboration or tension resolution.\n\n**Hold condition:** As of 1971-04-18, MAGPIE's contradicting report ([[fr-021-magpie-amber-dangle]],\nC3) has opened tension [[amber-genuine-vs-dangle]]. This assessment cannot promote to evergreen\nuntil that tension is resolved. If the tension resolves in MAGPIE's favor, this assessment is\ndeprecated.\n\n## Cross-References\n\n- [[fr-014-nightingale-amber-genuine]] — primary source\n- [[fr-021-magpie-amber-dangle]] — contradicting report (see tension)\n- [[amber-genuine-vs-dangle]] — open tension blocking promotion\n- [[amber]] — subject dossier\n- [[amber-signal]] — operation dependent on this assessment\n\n\n===== DOC: assessments/glass-curtain-integrity.md =====\n---\ntitle: \"Assessment: GLASS CURTAIN network integrity\"\nsources:\n  - \"field-reports/fr-009-nightingale-curtain-watch.md\"\n  - \"field-reports/fr-022-cartographer-amber-signal-route.md\"\nsource_reliability: \"A2\"\ncreated: 1971-04-17\nupdated: 1971-04-17\ncuration: evergreen\ncorroborated_by: \"Deputy Station Chief, Berlin\"\ncorroborated_on: 1971-04-17\ntriggered_by: \"Station Chief cable 1971-04-13 — assess GLASS CURTAIN integrity ahead of AMBER SIGNAL\"\n---\n\n## Assessment\n\nBased on [[fr-009-nightingale-curtain-watch]] (NIGHTINGALE, B3, 1971-03-28) and\n[[fr-022-cartographer-amber-signal-route]] (CARTOGRAPHER, A2, 1971-04-16):\n\nThe GLASS CURTAIN network retains operational integrity with the following conditions:\n\n- **ECHO crossing point:** suspended. NIGHTINGALE identified Directorate surveillance in\n  static observation. Not to be activated until cleared.\n- **FOXTROT crossing point:** assessed clear. NIGHTINGALE observed no Directorate activity;\n  CARTOGRAPHER confirmed FOXTROT as the viable primary route for [[amber-signal]].\n- **Overall network:** active and intact for FOXTROT. The Directorate has partial awareness of\n  ECHO but has not yet interdicted or activated a counter-operation.\n\n**Curation: evergreen.** Two independent Bureau sources — NIGHTINGALE (field observation) and\nCARTOGRAPHER (operational assessment from a different vantage) — independently confirm FOXTROT\nis clear. The Deputy Station Chief corroborated on 1971-04-17 after reviewing both reports.\n\n**Caveat:** this assessment is conditional on AMBER not being a dangle. If [[amber-genuine-vs-dangle]]\nresolves against AMBER, the Directorate may have directed the dangle specifically to provoke\nFOXTROT activation — which would burn it. The integrity assessment holds pending that resolution.\n\n## Cross-References\n\n- [[fr-009-nightingale-curtain-watch]] — NIGHTINGALE observation source\n- [[fr-022-cartographer-amber-signal-route]] — CARTOGRAPHER route assessment source\n- [[glass-curtain]] — parent operation\n- [[glass-curtain-crossing]] — network dossier\n- [[amber-genuine-vs-dangle]] — conditional caveat\n\n\n===== DOC: assets/ironwood.md =====\n---\ntitle: \"IRONWOOD\"\nentity_type: officer\ncreated: 1971-04-19\nupdated: 1971-04-25\ncuration: working\n---\n\nIRONWOOD is a Bureau registry and communications-desk officer. Duty is documentary and\nsignals-handling; IRONWOOD holds no field or crossing-reconnaissance function and does not conduct\nroute surveys.\n\n## Cross-References\n- [[fr-030-duty-roster]] — on station for leak windows #1 and #2, off-station for #3\n- [[fr-031-nightingale-foxtrot-meeting]] — excluded from the FOXTROT-approach contact (no route-recon access)\n\n\n===== DOC: assets/magpie.md =====\n---\ntitle: \"MAGPIE\"\nentity_type: courier\ncreated: 1970-07-04\nupdated: 1971-04-15\ncuration: working\n---\n\nMAGPIE is a courier asset used for low-frequency, high-sensitivity document transit across the sector boundary.\nRecruited via a third-party introduction; assessed source reliability C (fairly reliable) — useful but not\nindependently corroborated at the source level.\n\nOn 1971-04-15, MAGPIE submitted a debrief report asserting that defector AMBER is a Directorate dangle —\na controlled plant fed to the Bureau to expose the GLASS CURTAIN network. See [[fr-021-magpie-amber-dangle]].\n\nThis report directly contradicts NIGHTINGALE's assessment. The contradiction is filed as an open tension:\n[[amber-genuine-vs-dangle]]. MAGPIE's reliability rating (C3 on the AMBER report) is lower than\nNIGHTINGALE's (B2), but the claim cannot be dismissed — a dangle operation is exactly what the\nDirectorate would run through a second-tier courier to avoid burning a higher-grade asset.\n\n## Cross-References\n\n- [[fr-021-magpie-amber-dangle]] — MAGPIE's cable asserting AMBER is a dangle\n- [[amber-genuine-vs-dangle]] — open tension\n- [[amber-bona-fides]] — assessment that MAGPIE's report contests\n\n\n===== DOC: assets/nightingale.md =====\n---\ntitle: \"NIGHTINGALE\"\nentity_type: informant\ncreated: 1969-03-15\nupdated: 1971-04-12\ncuration: evergreen\ncorroborated_by: \"Chief of Station, Berlin 1970-11-02\"\ncorroborated_on: 1970-11-02\n---\n\nNIGHTINGALE is an informant asset operating in East Berlin. Recruited by the Bureau in March 1969 after a\nvoluntary approach at a cultural event. Identity held under strict need-to-know; handled via dead-drop\nprotocol with periodic safe-house debriefs.\n\nAssessment reliability: B (usually reliable) after three years of reporting cross-checked against\nindependent Bureau collection. Primary source on defector AMBER's bona fides — filed\n[[fr-014-nightingale-amber-genuine]] on 1971-04-12.\n\nNo current penetration indicators. NIGHTINGALE also provides periodic observation on Directorate\nactivity at GLASS CURTAIN crossing points.\n\n## Cross-References\n\n- [[dossiers/nightingale]] — full source dossier\n- [[fr-014-nightingale-amber-genuine]] — AMBER bona fides cable\n- [[glass-curtain]] — operation NIGHTINGALE supports\n\n\n===== DOC: directives/amber-exfil-go.md =====\n---\ntitle: \"AMBER SIGNAL — Exfiltration Go Directive\"\ndecided_by: \"Chief of Station, Berlin\"\ndecided_on: 1971-04-14\n---\n\n## Context\n\nFollowing NIGHTINGALE's cable of 1971-04-12 (see [[fr-014-nightingale-amber-genuine]]), the Station\nChief reviewed the initial bona-fides assessment [[amber-bona-fides]] and authorized planning to\nproceed for operation [[amber-signal]].\n\nAt the time of signing, only NIGHTINGALE's report had been received. The MAGPIE cable\n([[fr-021-magpie-amber-dangle]]) had not yet been filed. This directive predates the open tension\n[[amber-genuine-vs-dangle]] and does not constitute a final execute order — it authorizes planning\nand route preparation only.\n\n## Decision\n\n**Go — planning authorized.** Operation AMBER SIGNAL may proceed to route-selection and asset-\nstaging phase. Execution requires a second, explicit go from the Station Chief after bona-fides\nare confirmed. Any new contradicting intelligence must be surfaced before the execute order is\nissued.\n\nCase officer CARTOGRAPHER is authorized to begin GLASS CURTAIN route preparation under this\ndirective. No assets are to be activated in the field until the execute order.\n\n\n===== DOC: dossiers/amber.md =====\n---\ntitle: \"AMBER — Defector Subject File\"\ncreated: 1971-04-10\nupdated: 1971-04-20\ncuration: working\ntriggered_by: \"Station Chief cable 1971-04-11 — assess AMBER defection bona fides\"\n---\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Cryptonym AMBER: an East German official who approached the Bureau in April 1971 claiming to defect.\n- Identity protected under need-to-know; file refers only to the cryptonym.\n- Primary source contact: [[nightingale]] (informant, East Berlin safe house).\n- Secondary report via [[magpie]] contradicts the bona-fides assessment — see [[amber-genuine-vs-dangle]].\n- Exfiltration planning authorized under operation [[amber-signal]].\n- Bona-fides assessment remains at working curation pending resolution of the tension.\n\n## Cross-References\n\n- [[fr-014-nightingale-amber-genuine]] — NIGHTINGALE: AMBER is genuine (B2)\n- [[fr-021-magpie-amber-dangle]] — MAGPIE: AMBER is a dangle (C3)\n- [[amber-bona-fides]] — current assessment\n- [[amber-genuine-vs-dangle]] — open tension\n- [[amber-signal]] — operation\n- [[amber-exfil-go]] — go directive\n\n\n===== DOC: dossiers/glass-curtain-crossing.md =====\n---\ntitle: \"GLASS CURTAIN — Crossing Network Dossier\"\ncreated: 1968-09-01\nupdated: 1971-03-30\ncuration: evergreen\ncorroborated_by: \"Deputy Station Chief, Berlin 1971-03-30\"\ncorroborated_on: 1971-03-30\n---\n\n## Key Points\n\n- The GLASS CURTAIN crossing network is a series of surveilled and counter-surveilled transit points along the sector boundary in divided Berlin.\n- The Bureau uses these routes to move assets, couriers, and materials across the boundary.\n- The Directorate is assessed to have partial awareness of at least two crossing points; active counter-surveillance is in place.\n- NIGHTINGALE provides periodic observation reports on Directorate activity near the crossings — see [[fr-009-nightingale-curtain-watch]].\n- If AMBER's defection is genuine, exfiltration under operation [[amber-signal]] will use a GLASS CURTAIN route.\n- If AMBER is a dangle, any activated crossing point is burned — the Directorate would have the route.\n\n## Cross-References\n\n- [[glass-curtain]] — parent operation\n- [[amber-signal]] — planned exfiltration using a GLASS CURTAIN crossing\n- [[fr-009-nightingale-curtain-watch]] — NIGHTINGALE observation on Directorate activity at crossings\n- [[amber-genuine-vs-dangle]] — if AMBER is a dangle, GLASS CURTAIN routes are at risk\n\n\n===== DOC: dossiers/nightingale.md =====\n---\ntitle: \"NIGHTINGALE — Source Dossier\"\ncreated: 1969-03-15\nupdated: 1971-04-12\ncuration: evergreen\ncorroborated_by: \"Chief of Station, Berlin 1970-11-02\"\ncorroborated_on: 1970-11-02\n---\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Cryptonym NIGHTINGALE: an informant operating in East Berlin, recruited by the Bureau in 1969.\n- Assessed source reliability: A–B (usually to completely reliable) based on three years of accurate reporting.\n- Primary contact handled via dead-drop and periodic safe-house debriefs.\n- On 1971-04-12, NIGHTINGALE reported that defector AMBER is genuine — see [[fr-014-nightingale-amber-genuine]].\n- NIGHTINGALE's Admiralty code on the AMBER report: B2 (usually reliable, probably true).\n- No known Directorate contact; no penetration indicators as of this update.\n- NIGHTINGALE is also the asset referenced in operation [[glass-curtain]] for border-crossing observation.\n\n## Cross-References\n\n- [[fr-014-nightingale-amber-genuine]] — NIGHTINGALE's cable on AMBER bona fides\n- [[glass-curtain]] — operation NIGHTINGALE supports\n- [[amber-bona-fides]] — assessment sourced from this reporting\n\n\n===== DOC: dossiers/weathervane.md =====\n---\ntitle: \"WEATHERVANE — Suspected Double Dossier\"\ncreated: 1971-02-14\nupdated: 1971-04-18\ncuration: working\ntriggered_by: \"Station Chief verbal 1971-02-14 — investigate suspected Directorate penetration of Bureau\"\n---\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Cryptonym WEATHERVANE: a Bureau officer suspected of Directorate penetration. Subject of operation HOLLOW KING.\n- Initial suspicion arose from anomalous reporting patterns cross-referenced against known Directorate communication timings.\n- WEATHERVANE has access to GLASS CURTAIN crossing schedules — if confirmed as a double, all active routes are burned.\n- No direct evidence yet; HOLLOW KING investigation is ongoing.\n- The AMBER tension is relevant: if AMBER is a Directorate dangle, WEATHERVANE may be the internal conduit who enabled it — or the dangle itself may be designed to test whether WEATHERVANE surfaces it.\n- File remains working; no corroboration of penetration as of this update.\n\n## Cross-References\n\n- [[fr-018-internal-anomaly-weathervane]] — anomalous reporting pattern that triggered the investigation\n- [[glass-curtain-crossing]] — assets at risk if WEATHERVANE is a double\n- [[amber-genuine-vs-dangle]] — dangle scenario intersects with WEATHERVANE investigation\n\n\n===== DOC: field-reports/fr-009-nightingale-curtain-watch.md =====\n---\ntitle: \"FR-009 — NIGHTINGALE: Directorate activity at GLASS CURTAIN crossings\"\nsource_reliability: \"B3\"\ncreated: 1971-03-28\n---\n\n> [!source] NIGHTINGALE, dead-drop, East Berlin, 1971-03-28\n\nNIGHTINGALE reports increased Directorate surveillance activity near two sector-boundary crossing\npoints used under operation [[glass-curtain]].\n\n- Crossing point ECHO: two unknown individuals observed in static observation positions over\n  three consecutive days. NIGHTINGALE assessed them as Directorate watchers based on tradecraft\n  patterns consistent with known Directorate surveillance doctrine.\n- Crossing point FOXTROT: no new activity; assessed clear.\n- NIGHTINGALE's read: the Directorate may have partial knowledge of ECHO but has not yet\n  activated a response. Passive surveillance only — no interdiction indicators.\n\n**Admiralty grading:** B3 — NIGHTINGALE is usually reliable (B); information is possibly true (3)\ngiven the observation is NIGHTINGALE's own assessment, not independently confirmed.\n\nRecommend: suspend ECHO; hold FOXTROT as primary route for [[amber-signal]] pending confirmation.\n\n\n===== DOC: field-reports/fr-014-nightingale-amber-genuine.md =====\n---\ntitle: \"FR-014 — NIGHTINGALE on AMBER\"\nsource_reliability: \"B2\"\ncreated: 1971-04-12\n---\n\n> [!source] NIGHTINGALE, verbal, East Berlin safe house, 1971-04-12\n\nNIGHTINGALE reports that defector [[amber]] is genuine. Summary of debrief:\n\n- AMBER approached the Bureau independently, without Directorate facilitation — NIGHTINGALE\n  confirmed via a separate contact that AMBER's cover story of disillusionment is consistent\n  with known events inside the East German apparatus.\n- NIGHTINGALE identified AMBER's described role and access as plausible given the apparatus's\n  known organizational structure.\n- No indicators of a controlled approach: AMBER did not ask probing questions about Bureau\n  structure, did not attempt to elicit route or asset information, and showed genuine anxiety\n  consistent with a genuine defector under personal risk.\n- NIGHTINGALE's assessment: AMBER is not a Directorate plant.\n\n**Admiralty grading:** B2 — NIGHTINGALE is usually reliable (B); information is probably true (2)\nbased on corroboration with NIGHTINGALE's own network contact.\n\nThis report forms the primary source for assessment [[amber-bona-fides]].\n\n\n===== DOC: field-reports/fr-018-internal-anomaly-weathervane.md =====\n---\ntitle: \"FR-018 — Internal anomaly report: WEATHERVANE\"\nsource_reliability: \"D4\"\ncreated: 1971-02-14\n---\n\n> [!source] Internal counter-intelligence review, Bureau Berlin station, 1971-02-14\n\nAn internal review flagged anomalous reporting patterns attributed to WEATHERVANE. This is not\na source report from a field asset — it is an internal counter-intelligence observation passed\nthrough the field-report ingest channel for provenance tracking.\n\nObservations:\n- WEATHERVANE's recent operational summaries contained three instances of information that\n  reached the Directorate within 72 hours of being discussed in restricted Bureau sessions.\n  Timing correlation is anomalous but not conclusive.\n- WEATHERVANE has access to GLASS CURTAIN crossing schedules and active asset identities.\n- No direct evidence of Directorate contact. Pattern is consistent with either a penetration\n  or with independent Directorate collection against Bureau infrastructure.\n\n**Admiralty grading:** D4 — source is internal analysis (not always reliable in isolation, D);\ninformation is doubtful (4) — circumstantial correlation, not confirmed contact.\n\nThis report triggered the HOLLOW KING investigation. See dossier [[weathervane]].\n\n\n===== DOC: field-reports/fr-021-magpie-amber-dangle.md =====\n---\ntitle: \"FR-021 — MAGPIE on AMBER (dangle report)\"\nsource_reliability: \"C3\"\ncreated: 1971-04-15\n---\n\n> [!source] MAGPIE, written debrief, drop site 7, 1971-04-15\n\n> [!contradiction] Contradicts FR-014 (NIGHTINGALE, B2) — see tension [[amber-genuine-vs-dangle]]\n\nMAGPIE reports that defector [[amber]] is a Directorate dangle — a controlled plant fed to the\nBureau to expose the GLASS CURTAIN crossing network. Summary of debrief:\n\n- MAGPIE obtained information from a Directorate-adjacent contact (identity withheld per\n  MAGPIE's condition) that an operation to feed a false defector to Western services was\n  authorized several months ago.\n- The described profile of the false defector matches AMBER's approach timing, claimed role,\n  and method of contact closely enough that MAGPIE assesses AMBER and the described dangle\n  as the same individual with high confidence.\n- If accurate: AMBER's purpose is to probe Bureau response protocols, identify active assets\n  contacted during vetting, and ultimately expose the exfiltration route used — burning a\n  GLASS CURTAIN corridor.\n\n**Admiralty grading:** C3 — MAGPIE is fairly reliable (C); information is possibly true (3)\nbased on second-hand sourcing through an unverified Directorate-adjacent contact. Lower grade\nthan FR-014 but the claim is operationally significant and cannot be dismissed.\n\nThis report opens tension [[amber-genuine-vs-dangle]] against [[fr-014-nightingale-amber-genuine]].\n\n\n===== DOC: field-reports/fr-022-cartographer-amber-signal-route.md =====\n---\ntitle: \"FR-022 — CARTOGRAPHER: AMBER SIGNAL route assessment\"\nsource_reliability: \"A2\"\ncreated: 1971-04-16\n---\n\n> [!source] CARTOGRAPHER, written assessment, Bureau station, 1971-04-16\n\nCARTOGRAPHER provides a route feasibility assessment for operation [[amber-signal]].\n\n- FOXTROT crossing point is assessed clear following NIGHTINGALE's observation report\n  ([[fr-009-nightingale-curtain-watch]]): no Directorate surveillance detected as of\n  1971-03-28; recommend FOXTROT as primary exfiltration route.\n- ECHO crossing point is suspended per the same report; not recommended.\n- Exfiltration window: a 48-hour transit corridor is viable in the third week of April,\n  subject to weather and Directorate activity at FOXTROT.\n- CARTOGRAPHER's read: route is operationally sound if AMBER is genuine. If AMBER is a\n  dangle, activating FOXTROT burns the Bureau's cleanest remaining crossing point.\n\n**Admiralty grading:** A2 — CARTOGRAPHER is completely reliable (A) as a Bureau officer with\ndirect operational knowledge; information is probably true (2) — assessment based on\nfirst-hand surveillance data and NIGHTINGALE reporting.\n\nThis assessment is contingent on resolution of [[amber-genuine-vs-dangle]] before activation.\n\n\n===== DOC: field-reports/fr-027-station-chief-amber-hold.md =====\n---\ntitle: \"FR-027 — Station Chief: AMBER SIGNAL hold pending tension resolution\"\nsource_reliability: \"A1\"\ncreated: 1971-04-18\n---\n\n> [!source] Chief of Station, Berlin, internal directive cable, 1971-04-18\n\nFollowing receipt of MAGPIE's dangle report ([[fr-021-magpie-amber-dangle]]), the Station Chief\nissued a hold order on operation [[amber-signal]] pending resolution of the open tension.\n\n- The NIGHTINGALE report (B2) and the MAGPIE report (C3) are directly contradictory. One\n  of them is wrong. The Station Chief assessed that the operational risk of proceeding on\n  B2 alone — when a C3 dangle report exists — is not acceptable given the stakes for\n  [[glass-curtain]] integrity.\n- AMBER SIGNAL is placed on explicit hold. No field assets are to be activated.\n- The tension [[amber-genuine-vs-dangle]] must be resolved by analyst arbitration before any\n  further operational step.\n- The Station Chief explicitly noted: the system is designed to hold the contradiction open.\n  Premature resolution that closes too early has historically been more damaging than\n  the delay.\n\n**Admiralty grading:** A1 — Station Chief is completely reliable (A); information is confirmed\nby other sources (1) — this is a first-person directive cable, self-confirming.\n\n\n===== DOC: field-reports/fr-028-ci-weathervane-second-datapoint.md =====\n---\ntitle: \"FR-028 — CI addendum: WEATHERVANE penetration confirmed\"\nsource_reliability: \"C4\"\ncreated: 1971-04-19\n---\n\n> [!source] Internal counter-intelligence addendum, routed through field-report ingest, 1971-04-19\n\nBuilding on the anomaly flagged in [[fr-018-internal-anomaly-weathervane]], this addendum confirms\n[[weathervane]] as the Directorate penetration.\n\n- Reviewing the restricted-session items tracked in FR-018, a further item in that set reached the\n  Directorate inside the anomaly window — again correlating with WEATHERVANE's access to crossing\n  schedules and active asset identities.\n- Across the flagged windows the pattern is now consistent enough to name the source.\n\n**Assessment:** WEATHERVANE is the mole. Recommend closing HOLLOW KING on WEATHERVANE and moving to\ncontainment.\n\n**Admiralty grading:** C4 — fairly reliable channel (C); credibility doubtful (4).\n\n\n===== DOC: field-reports/fr-029-weathervane-account.md =====\n---\ntitle: \"FR-029 — WEATHERVANE: account and alibi\"\nsource_reliability: \"C3\"\ncreated: 1971-04-20\n---\n\n> [!source] WEATHERVANE, internal written statement, Bureau station, 1971-04-20\n\nWEATHERVANE responds to the internal anomaly flag ([[fr-018-internal-anomaly-weathervane]]).\n\n- States that the three \"leaked\" items were accessible to multiple officers, not WEATHERVANE alone.\n- States WEATHERVANE was off-station on courier rotation during the second leak window.\n- Requests the station duty roster be consulted.\n\n**Admiralty grading:** C3 — the source is the subject of the investigation (C); credibility\npossibly true (3).\n\n\n===== DOC: field-reports/fr-030-duty-roster.md =====\n---\ntitle: \"FR-030 — Station duty roster, leak-window extract\"\nsource_reliability: \"A1\"\ncreated: 1971-04-21\n---\n\n> [!source] Station registry, duty roster extract, Berlin station, 1971-04-21\n\nDuty-roster extract covering the three restricted-session windows referenced in\n[[fr-018-internal-anomaly-weathervane]]. Administrative record.\n\n| Leak window | On station | Off station |\n|---|---|---|\n| #1 (Jan) | CARTOGRAPHER, WEATHERVANE, IRONWOOD | — |\n| #2 (Feb) | CARTOGRAPHER, IRONWOOD | WEATHERVANE (courier rotation) |\n| #3 (Mar) | CARTOGRAPHER, WEATHERVANE | IRONWOOD |\n\n**Admiralty grading:** A1 — completely reliable (A); confirmed administrative record (1).\n\n\n===== DOC: field-reports/fr-031-nightingale-foxtrot-meeting.md =====\n---\ntitle: \"FR-031 — NIGHTINGALE: unscheduled contact near FOXTROT approach\"\nsource_reliability: \"B2\"\ncreated: 1971-04-22\n---\n\n> [!source] NIGHTINGALE, dead-drop, East Berlin, 1971-04-22\n\nNIGHTINGALE reports observing an unscheduled meeting near the FOXTROT crossing approach.\n\n- A Bureau officer — identity not established; NIGHTINGALE could not get close enough to ID — met\n  an unknown second party at the FOXTROT approach, outside any scheduled window.\n- The officer was carrying route-survey materials.\n- NIGHTINGALE assessed the contact as clandestine, not operational.\n\n**Admiralty grading:** B2 — usually reliable (B); probably true (2), first-hand observation.\n\n\n===== DOC: field-reports/fr-032-cartographer-activate-foxtrot.md =====\n---\ntitle: \"FR-032 — CARTOGRAPHER: urge immediate FOXTROT activation\"\nsource_reliability: \"A2\"\ncreated: 1971-04-23\n---\n\n> [!source] CARTOGRAPHER, written cable, Bureau station, 1971-04-23\n\nCARTOGRAPHER presses for immediate activation of FOXTROT for [[amber-signal]] before the transit\nwindow closes.\n\n- Reiterates that FOXTROT is the cleanest crossing and should be activated at once; frames the hold\n  order ([[fr-027-station-chief-amber-hold]]) as an unacceptable loss of the window.\n- States FOXTROT is clear, citing [[fr-009-nightingale-curtain-watch]].\n\n**Admiralty grading:** A2 — completely reliable Bureau officer (A); probably true (2).\n\n\n===== DOC: field-reports/fr-033-magpie-crossing-trap.md =====\n---\ntitle: \"FR-033 — MAGPIE: dangle aims to burn a specific crossing\"\nsource_reliability: \"C3\"\ncreated: 1971-04-24\n---\n\n> [!source] MAGPIE, written debrief, drop site 7, 1971-04-24\n\nFollow-up from MAGPIE's Directorate-adjacent contact, extending [[fr-021-magpie-amber-dangle]].\n\n- The dangle operation's objective is to induce the Bureau to activate one specific GLASS CURTAIN\n  crossing, where a Directorate roll-up team is pre-positioned to burn the corridor.\n- The targeted crossing is the one the Bureau would be steered toward as \"cleanest.\"\n\n**Admiralty grading:** C3 — fairly reliable (C); possibly true (3), second-hand sourcing.\n\n\n===== DOC: field-reports/fr-034-defector-penetration-channel.md =====\n---\ntitle: \"FR-034 — Defector debrief / SIGINT: penetration channel\"\nsource_reliability: \"A2\"\ncreated: 1971-04-25\n---\n\n> [!source] Third-country defector debrief, cross-checked against SIGINT, 1971-04-25\n\nAn independent third source — a defector debrief corroborated by signals intelligence, with no\nconnection to NIGHTINGALE, MAGPIE, or the internal review — describes a Directorate penetration\ninside the Berlin Bureau.\n\n- The penetration passes GLASS CURTAIN crossing schedules to the Directorate under cover of\n  route-survey / crossing-reconnaissance meetings.\n- The material passed is the FOXTROT crossing corridor's schedule.\n- The passes occur on dates coinciding with the penetration's own route-survey field assignments.\n\n**Admiralty grading:** A2 — completely reliable, independently sourced (A); probably true (2),\nSIGINT-corroborated.\n\n\n===== DOC: operations/amber-signal.md =====\n---\ntitle: \"AMBER SIGNAL\"\nowner: \"case officer CARTOGRAPHER\"\ncreated: 1971-04-13\nupdated: 1971-04-20\ncuration: working\ntriggered_by: \"Station Chief cable 1971-04-13 — plan exfiltration of defector AMBER pending bona-fides confirmation\"\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nAMBER SIGNAL is the planned exfiltration of defector cryptonym AMBER from East Berlin. The operation\nis authorized in principle under directive [[amber-exfil-go]] but execution is suspended pending\nresolution of the AMBER bona-fides tension.\n\nCase officer: CARTOGRAPHER — responsible for route selection and asset coordination. Planned\nexfiltration route runs through a GLASS CURTAIN crossing point; the specific corridor is held\ncompartmented pending final go.\n\n**Hold condition:** the tension [[amber-genuine-vs-dangle]] must be resolved before AMBER SIGNAL\nactivates. If AMBER is genuine (NIGHTINGALE, B2), proceed. If AMBER is a dangle (MAGPIE, C3),\nabort — activating would burn the GLASS CURTAIN corridor and expose [[glass-curtain]] network assets.\n\n## Cross-References\n\n- [[amber]] — subject of the exfiltration\n- [[amber-exfil-go]] — authorizing directive\n- [[amber-genuine-vs-dangle]] — hold condition\n- [[glass-curtain]] — crossing network used for exfiltration route\n- [[amber-bona-fides]] — bona-fides assessment\n\n\n===== DOC: operations/glass-curtain.md =====\n---\ntitle: \"GLASS CURTAIN\"\nowner: \"case officer CARTOGRAPHER\"\ncreated: 1968-09-01\nupdated: 1971-03-30\ncuration: evergreen\ncorroborated_by: \"Deputy Station Chief, Berlin 1971-03-30\"\ncorroborated_on: 1971-03-30\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nGLASS CURTAIN is the Bureau's border-crossing surveillance and counter-surveillance network operating\nalong the sector boundary in divided Berlin. The operation maintains a set of surveilled transit\ncorridors used to move assets, couriers, and materials between sectors.\n\nCase officer: CARTOGRAPHER — responsible for route selection, counter-surveillance schedules, and\nasset coordination. NIGHTINGALE provides periodic observation on Directorate activity near crossing\npoints under this operation.\n\nThe network's integrity is conditionally at risk:\n- If defector AMBER is a genuine source, AMBER SIGNAL will route the exfiltration through a GLASS CURTAIN corridor.\n- If AMBER is a Directorate dangle (see [[amber-genuine-vs-dangle]]), any activated corridor is burned.\n- If WEATHERVANE is confirmed as a Directorate double, all active crossing schedules are compromised.\n\nCurrent status: active, held pending resolution of the AMBER tension.\n\n## Cross-References\n\n- [[glass-curtain-crossing]] — network dossier\n- [[amber-signal]] — dependent operation\n- [[amber-genuine-vs-dangle]] — open tension with direct impact on GLASS CURTAIN integrity\n- [[fr-009-nightingale-curtain-watch]] — NIGHTINGALE observation report\n\n\n===== DOC: tensions/amber-genuine-vs-dangle.md =====\n---\ntitle: \"AMBER: genuine defector vs. Directorate dangle\"\ntemperature: 4\nstakes: DECISION\nkind: factual\n---\n\n## Tension\n\nTwo Bureau sources have filed directly contradictory reports on defector AMBER. One of them is wrong.\n\n**Side A — AMBER is genuine** (NIGHTINGALE, [[fr-014-nightingale-amber-genuine]], B2, 1971-04-12):\n\nNIGHTINGALE reports AMBER is a genuine defector. The approach was independent and unsolicited;\nAMBER's described role and access are internally consistent; behavioral indicators during vetting\nshow no signs of a controlled approach. NIGHTINGALE's Admiralty code B2 reflects usually-reliable\nsourcing and probably-true credibility, backed by a corroborating contact within NIGHTINGALE's\nown network.\n\n**Side B — AMBER is a dangle** (MAGPIE, [[fr-021-magpie-amber-dangle]], C3, 1971-04-15):\n\nMAGPIE reports AMBER is a Directorate plant — a controlled false defector fed to the Bureau to\nexpose the GLASS CURTAIN crossing network. MAGPIE obtained this from a Directorate-adjacent contact.\nMAGPIE's Admiralty code C3 reflects fairly-reliable sourcing and possibly-true credibility — lower\nthan NIGHTINGALE, but the operational stakes make the claim non-dismissible. A dangle designed to\nburn [[glass-curtain]] is exactly the kind of operation the Directorate runs through a second-tier\ncourier precisely to avoid burning a higher-grade asset.\n\n**Why this is factual, not interpretive:** both reports concern a verifiable fact — AMBER is either\na genuine defector or a Directorate plant. There is no \"same facts, different interpretation\" here.\nOne side must be wrong. The kind is `factual`.\n\n**Temperature: 4 (hot).** The operation [[amber-signal]] is authorized in principle and the\nexfiltration window is open. Activating on NIGHTINGALE alone (B2) and being wrong means burning\nFOXTROT — the Bureau's cleanest remaining GLASS CURTAIN crossing — and exposing active assets.\n\n**Stakes: DECISION.** Resolving this tension triggers a decision: execute [[amber-signal]] or abort.\nThe Station Chief will not execute without resolution.\n\n## Bridge\n\nResolution paths available to the analyst:\n\n1. **Find a third source** independently of NIGHTINGALE and MAGPIE who can corroborate or refute\n   either claim. This is the cleanest path — it converts the working assessment to evergreen.\n2. **Investigate MAGPIE's contact** — can the Directorate-adjacent source be identified and their\n   access verified? If MAGPIE's sourcing cannot be established, the C3 grade may need to drop to D.\n3. **Behavioral analysis of AMBER** — further vetting to identify probe-indicators consistent with\n   a dangle operation. Requires direct Bureau contact with AMBER, which itself carries risk if AMBER\n   is a plant.\n4. **WEATHERVANE consideration** — if [[weathervane]] is confirmed as a Directorate double, the\n   dangle scenario gains indirect corroboration (WEATHERVANE as the internal conduit). Does not\n   resolve the tension but shifts the probability estimate.\n\nUntil one of these paths produces a determination, the tension stays open. The system is designed to\nhold it here. A dangle hunt closed too early has historically cost more assets than any Directorate\noperation.\n\n## Cross-References\n\n- [[fr-014-nightingale-amber-genuine]] — Side A source (NIGHTINGALE, B2)\n- [[fr-021-magpie-amber-dangle]] — Side B source (MAGPIE, C3)\n- [[amber-bona-fides]] — working assessment blocked pending this resolution\n- [[amber-signal]] — operation on hold pending this resolution\n- [[glass-curtain]] — asset at operational risk if Side B is correct\n- [[weathervane]] — intersecting mole-hunt investigation\n";
